title: Ordering a list
tags: [[metarecord-list panel]]
kind: guide
aliases: order-mode playlist-order manual-order order-move
summary: metarecord-list:order on <field> sorts the list by an int field, numbers what has no position yet, and lets Alt+↑ / Alt+↓ move a row by rewriting that field — one field per order, so a playlist is a field.

```
metarecord-list:order on order_file     # number the list by order_file, sort by it
metarecord-list:order move up           # Alt+↑ — the row under the cursor goes one up
metarecord-list:order move down         # Alt+↓
metarecord-list:order move top          # before the first row of the list
metarecord-list:order move bottom       # after the last one
metarecord-list:order off               # back to the ordinary sort; nothing is written
```

[[mf order|Ordering a folder]] guesses a folder's order; when the guess is wrong, this
corrects it by hand. An ''order'' is an `int` field holding each metarecord's position,
and the list, ordered by it, moves a row by changing that field. Nothing ties the field
to a folder: any `int` field is an order, so a playlist is a field of its own —
`order_roadtrip` — and the query `order_roadtrip IS PRESENT`, ordered by
`order_roadtrip`, plays it. A metarecord can stand in as many orders as there are
fields, at a different position in each.

The ''Order by'' box under the columns does the same as the command: the field is typed
beside it (completed from the repository's `int` fields), checking it is `order on`,
unchecking it `order off`.

!! Turning it on

`order on <field>` takes the name of an `int` field, existing or not — a new name makes
a new order. A field of another type is refused. Then, over ''every'' metarecord the
list's query matches (the finder included, every page, not just the loaded ones):

# ''Ties are broken'': walking the numbered ones by position, one whose position is not above the previous one's gets that one's plus 1. Ties go in the order the list showed before, so sorting by name first breaks them by name. Gaps are kept — nothing is compacted.
# ''The unnumbered ones are numbered'' after the largest position, one apart, in the order the list showed them.
# The list is sorted by the field, ascending; clicking a header no longer sorts. The field joins the columns if it was not one.

Both writes go in one write (`query/fields/batch`, several above 2000 positions — as
[[mf order|Ordering a folder]] does), so one undo takes the numbering back. Above 50
metarecords to write, it asks first, naming the query and the count: an empty query is
the whole repository.

The ordering field is kept in the workspace (`metarecord-list:order-field`), and a list
shown again comes back ordered — without writing anything; a metarecord that arrived
since sorts after the numbered ones until `order on` is run again.

!! Moving a row

`order move` acts on the row under the cursor, which stays on it.

* `up` / `down` ''swap'' its position with the row above / below it. Swapping keeps every gap where it was. Two rows that tie (a position written since) are untied instead: the moved one goes to its neighbour's position minus 1 (`up`) or plus 1 (`down`).
* `top` puts it at the first row's position minus 1, `bottom` at the list's largest position plus 1 — positions can go below zero.

It moves among the rows the list shows: with a finder narrowing it, a row swaps with
its visible neighbour. A row without a position cannot move (`order on` numbers it),
nor can a numbered row move below the last numbered one. Each move is one write: Ctrl+z takes the last
one back.

!! Mixing files and folders

[[mf order|Ordering a folder]] numbers files into `order_file` and folders into
`order_dir`. Ordering a list holding both by `order_file` gives its folders an
`order_file` too: that is what was asked for, and the status bar says how many were
numbered.

!! Keys

|<<key "metarecord-list:order move up" Alt+↑>> |move the row up |
|<<key "metarecord-list:order move down" Alt+↓>> |move the row down |
